// Four-bar singular worked example
//
// Demonstrates the kinematic-singularity diagnostic (task 3580 / KCC-alpha,
// GR-039): a closed four-body loop whose loop-closure Jacobian is
// rank-deficient at the modeled configuration.
//
// Four-bar framing: four bodies (solidA, solidB, solidC, solidD) form ONE
// closed loop, closed by a single redundant edge.  solidA and solidB each
// hang directly off world (via j_a and j_b respectively); solidC re-anchors
// j_x on top of solidA's frame (spanning-tree edge); solidD re-anchors the
// SAME j_x on top of solidB's frame (closing edge) — this is the loop's
// closing joint pair: j_x / j_b.  Binding only j_a leaves both j_b and j_x
// free, and both act along the SAME +X axis, so their finite-difference
// Jacobian columns are identical → rank-1 JᵀJ → NewtonOutcome::Singular.
//
// j_a/j_b/j_x use three DIFFERENT ranges (rather than three identical
// prismatic(vec3(1,0,0), 0mm .. 1000mm) calls) so that:
//   1. Their Values are structurally distinct — identical-range joints
//      would be byte-identical Value::Maps that alias in joint_parents,
//      collapsing the intended 2-free-joint/1-loop-closure topology into a
//      spurious second closure.
//   2. The Newton closure residual is non-zero at the starting guess — j_b's
//      range (0..2100mm, midpoint 1050mm) never collides with j_a's range
//      (0..1000mm), so Newton must actually invert the rank-deficient
//      Jacobian rather than converging trivially at iteration 0.
//
// Topology mirrors
// crates/reify-eval/tests/kinematic_singularity_snapshot_e2e.rs::SINGULAR_SOURCE
// (task 3580's proven end-to-end fixture for this exact solver code path).
//
// MAINTENANCE: this body/joint wiring is a deliberate duplicate of
// SINGULAR_SOURCE (committed example vs. inline test fixture — see rationale
// above). If the solver's singularity conditions ever change in a way that
// requires editing one topology, update the other in the SAME change so
// neither fixture silently drifts into testing a stale/no-longer-singular
// chain; both tests assert is_singular=true and will fail loudly if you miss
// one, but keeping them in lockstep is currently a manual step.
//
// PRD ref: docs/prds/v0_3/kinematic-constraints-completion.md (KCC-iota, task 3848)
structure def FourBarSingular {
    let j_a = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 1000mm)
    let j_b = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 2100mm)
    let j_x = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 3000mm)

    let m0 = mechanism()
    let m1 = body(m0, "solidA", j_a)
    let m2 = body(m1, "solidB", j_b)
    let m3 = body(m2, "solidC", j_x, j_a)
    let m4 = body(m3, "solidD", j_x, j_b)

    // Loop joint pair: j_x (spanning-tree edge, anchored via solidC on top
    // of j_a's frame) / j_b (closing edge, re-anchored via solidD) — the
    // redundant closing edge that makes this loop singular.
    let snap = snapshot(m4, [bind(j_a, 500mm)])
}
